1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Nc3 a6 ({Whether the move the in the text is first
played, or} 3... Nc6 {, is of no importance.}) 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. a3 {
A "provincial" move. White tries to prevent his opponent making a bad move,
Bb4.} Nf6 7. Be3 Be7 (7... d5 {was preferable.}) 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 Qc7 10. Bg5
{White's 10th move is weak, but Black's reply still more so.} Ng4 11. Bxe7 Nxe7
12. h3 Nh6 13. O-O f5 14. Qe2 fxe4 ({Playing the opponent's game.} 14... Ng6 {
was the right rejoinder.}) 15. Nxe4 Ng6 16. Rfe1 Nf4 17. Qd1 Nxd3 18. Qxd3 b5
19. Neg5 g6 20. Re4 Nf5 21. Rg4 Bb7 22. Rd1 Bc6 23. Nd4 h5 24. Ngxe6 {A
brilliant and sound coup: White must have a won game, after a series of [ex]
changes, with the odds of the exchange and three pawns.} dxe6 25. Rxg6+ Kh8 26.
Nxe6 Qf7 27. Nxf8 Rxf8 28. Rxc6 Nh4 29. Rh6+ Kg8 30. Qg3+ Qg7 31. Qxg7+ Kxg7
32. Rxh5 {Source: Westminster Papers, 1 May 1876} 1-0

