1. d4 e6 2. e4 Nc6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Bd3 dxe4 5. Bxe4 h6 6. O-O Nf6 7. Bd3 Bd6 8. a3
b6 9. c4 Bb7 10. b4 O-O 11. Bb2 Ne7 {The opening moves on both sides show such
deficiency in the way to conduct a close game that it would be useless to
point out their especial weaknesses. On the whole, this is an illustration
that half knowledge is worse than complete ignorance.} 12. c5 bxc5 13. bxc5 ({
Ruinous:} 13. dxc5 {would provide White with a fine array of pawns on the
queen's side, and a superior position of pieces.}) 13... Bf4 14. g3 {Just what
Black wanted to obtain a powerful attack.} Qd5 15. Be2 Nh5 16. gxf4 (16. Nc3
Qc6 17. d5 {would break the adverse attack.}) 16... Nxf4 17. Nbd2 Neg6 18. Kh1
Nh4 19. Rg1 Nxe2 20. Qxe2 Nxf3 21. Nxf3 Qxf3+ 22. Qxf3 Bxf3+ 23. Rg2 Bxg2+ {
Played in a spirited manner, but the concluding move is poor. Could the white
rook escape?} 24. Kxg2 Rab8 25. Bc3 Rb3 26. Bb4 c6 {Source: Westminster Papers,
1 May 1876} 0-1

