1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Nf6 5. Bg5 {The usual continuations are
5.e5, 5.Ng5 and 5.0-0. The move chosen by White is not good.} Bb4+ ({I prefer
the simple rejoinder,} 5... h6) 6. c3 dxc3 7. bxc3 Bd6 {This move is better
than it looks. The bishop prevents the advance of the e-pawn, and the
temporary confinement of his companion in arms is not of vital importance.} 8.
O-O Ne5 ({Suicidal:} 8... h6 {was the only available resource.}) 9. Nxe5 Bxe5
10. f4 h6 11. Bh4 {Fine chess: this retreat is much better than the capture of
the piece. White must now gain a piece, and a superiority in position.} Bxf4
12. Rxf4 g5 13. Qd4 gxh4 14. Rxf6 Rh7 15. Nd2 d6 16. Raf1 d5 17. Qxd5 ({
White played the middlegame in vigorous style, but he misses the opportunity
to finish the game, by} 17. Rxf7) 17... Qxd5 18. Bxd5 Bd7 19. Bxf7+ Ke7 20. Bg6
Rg7 21. Rf7+ Rxf7 22. Rxf7+ Ke6 23. Nb3 b6 24. Nd4+ Kd6 25. e5+ Kxe5 26. Rxd7 {
"and wins" Source: Westminster Papers, 1 May 1876.} 1-0

