1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 Bd6 6. O-O O-O 7. h3 h6 8. Be3
({I prefer} 8. Nc3) 8... Ne4 {Premature. Black ought to develop his forces on
the queen's flank before engaging in any attack.} 9. c3 Qf6 10. Bxe4 ({Against
the principles of the game.} 10. Nbd2 {was the right rejoinder.}) 10... dxe4
11. Nh2 Qg6 12. Kh1 f5 13. f4 Nd7 14. Nd2 Nf6 15. Nc4 Nd5 16. Qd2 Qg3 17. Rae1
g5 18. Nxd6 cxd6 19. fxg5 Nxe3 20. Rxe3 Qxg5 21. Qf2 Kh7 22. Rg3 Qe7 23. Qf4 ({
If} 23. Ng4 {, Black would reply with advantage} f4) 23... Rg8 ({Why not} 23...
Bd7 {? The move in the text loses a valuable pawn.}) 24. Rxg8 Kxg8 25. Qxh6 Be6
26. Qg6+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Kg8 28. a3 ({Protecting a pawn, which could not be taken
if White played} 28. Re1 {(} Bxa2 $2 29. Re3 {). This timidity might be
explained by the desire to maintain the numerical superiority for the
approaching decision by the umpire.}) 28... Re8 29. Re1 Qg7 30. Qf4 d5 31. Re3
Kf7 32. Ng4 {adjudicated win for White (Source: Westminster Papers, 1 May 1876)
} ({A strong coup, and preferable to} 32. Rg3 {, which would be met by} Qf6 {
. The game was, at this stage, decided by the umpire in favour of White. White
ought to win; his position is much superior, and the passed h-pawn should
prove irresistible.}) (32. Ng4 Ke7 33. Qc7+ Kf8 34. Qd6+ Qe7 35. Qf4 {was some
of the analysis given in The Field, 8 April 1876}) 1-0

