1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 (5... Nxe4 {is better, and
leads, in a few moves, to an even game, whilst 5...Be7 leaves Black with a
cramped position.}) 6. Nc3 {The best reply.} d6 7. d4 ({I prefer} 7. Bxc6+ bxc6
8. h3 O-O 9. d3 {, which leads, by a transposition of moves, to Anderssen's
favourite variation of the Ruy Lopez.}) 7... exd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 9. Re1 O-O (9...
Nxd4 {was preferable.}) 10. Nxc6 Bxc6 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. Qf3 Nd7 13. Bf4 Rb8 14.
Rad1 Bf6 ({Vainly trying to prevent the advance of the adverse e-pawn. Why not
} 14... Rxb2 {?}) 15. e5 dxe5 16. Bg3 {This and the preceding moves were well
calculated. Black's pawns must now fall.} Qe7 17. Qxc6 Nb6 18. f4 Rfe8 19. fxe5
Bg5 20. a4 Na8 21. Nd5 Qf8 22. e6 fxe6 23. Rxe6 ({White could obtain a winning
attack by} 23. Rf1 Bf6 24. Nxf6+ gxf6 25. Be5) 23... Rxe6 24. Qxe6+ Qf7 25.
Qxa6 Qf8 26. Rd3 {To no purpose.} Rxb2 27. Qe6+ Kh8 28. Ne3 Rb1+ 29. Nd1 Rb8
30. Qd5 h6 31. a5 Rd8 32. Qc4 Rc8 33. Rd7 Qe8 34. Qf7 Qxf7 35. Rxf7 Bd2 36. Be5
Re8 {A good reply: the capture of the g-pawn would cost White a piece.} 37. Bc3
Bxc3 38. Nxc3 Kg8 39. Rf1 Re5 40. Ra1 Kf8 41. a6 Re8 42. a7 Ke7 43. Rb1 Nb6 44.
Re1+ Kd7 45. Rxe8 Kxe8 46. Kf2 Kd7 47. Kg3 Kc8 48. Kf4 (48. Nd5 {would have
shortened the contest.}) 48... Kb7 49. Kf5 Kxa7 50. Kg6 Nc4 51. Nd1 Kb6 52.
Kxg7 Kc6 53. Kxh6 Kd6 54. g4 Ke6 55. g5 Kf7 56. g6+ Kg8 57. Nf2 Nd6 58. Ng4
Nf5+ 59. Kg5 {Source: Westminster Papers, 1 May 1876} 1-0

